WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:02.040
- [Narrator] A wave of development

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.120
is rippling through the windy

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.870
and sunny parts of the United States.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.690
In the Midwest, newer
and bigger wind turbines

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.580
are rankling the locals,

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.810
and fights over solar
farms have broken out

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.630
in California and Virginia as well.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.780
In these fights property rights advocates

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.230
and environmentalists have joined forces

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:23.050
to push back against clean energy.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.580
New research says that if
this movement continues,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.290
it could stall the country's
green energy transition.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.880
- We used to think that
there's sufficient land

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.790
for the wind that is needed,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.950
but now we believe that there could be

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.280
some land use conflicts
that would actually hit

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.660
some of the limits to wind development.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.430
- [Narrator] The fights
about solar and wind

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.970
are all about how land is used.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.810
In several parts of rural America,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.750
locals say that the installations

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.560
are dominating the landscapes.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.340
- So these projects are as big as they are

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.850
because that's what makes
them as cheap as they are.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:54.683
- [Narrator] Samantha Gross,

00:00:54.683 --> 00:00:57.990
who studies energy security
at the Brookings Institution

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.460
says that, "Most US wind turbines
are roughly 500 feet tall

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.650
and are spaced more
than half a mile apart.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.620
These installations
can be seen for miles."

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.390
- You have projects being
put in place in those areas

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.250
where people aren't used to
seeing a lot of development,

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.660
and they're there because
that's where they need to be,

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.570
but they can generate public opposition

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.720
because they're a real change
to the use of the land.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.577
- [Narrator] Researchers say that,

00:01:22.577 --> 00:01:24.810
"It takes at least 10 times as much land

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.410
to develop renewables when
compared to fossil fuels."

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.470
- Well, if you think
about making electricity

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:34.230
from natural gas and the kind
of land footprint that has,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.590
it's about one 10th of the
land area, per unit of power

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.900
that you have for a wind facility,

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.530
You can do other things with that land.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.720
You can farm underneath it,
have it for pasture land,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.520
but still that land will be
occupied by the wind facility.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.540
- [Narrator] Despite
these land use issues,

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.430
financiers and leaders in Washington

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.290
are pouring money into
new green energy projects.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.310
- What we as a country should do

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.730
is make smart, sustainable investments

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.210
with appropriate financing

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.260
to make this nation more productive,

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:11.560
to advance America's
leadership in clean energy,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.840
to win the jobs of the future,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.910
or mitigate the threat of climate change.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.910
- [Narrator] The high level
of interest in clean energy

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.020
is causing controversy as the rubber

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.430
hits the road out in the country.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.370
Several states in the US wind quarter

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:25.860
are grappling with how to deploy

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.170
more renewables for the grid.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.280
The state of Indiana started to experiment

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.519
with utility scale wind
power in 2008, as of 2021,

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:36.970
40 of the state's 92 counties

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.000
have some amount of wind
or solar development,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.460
but a groundswell of activism

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.910
has taken over county
and state level politics.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.940
Citizens across Indiana have campaigned

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.120
against new clean energy developments,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.520
arguing that the structures
are an economic scam

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.430
that will be a nuisance to public health,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.430
biodiversity, and local property values.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.700
The movement gained
influence over the years.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.000
As of 2021, more than 30 Indiana counties

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.343
have restricted the development
of utility scale renewables.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.550
Many use setback requirements,

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.770
which determine how far away
projects must be removed

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.730
from existing properties.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.470
This tactic can keep
turbines away from homes

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.280
which reduces the flickering shadows

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.633
and noise created by the installations.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.700
But some residents would
like to see the bans

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.070
on renewables come to an end.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.107
- There are folks who were saying,

00:03:27.107 --> 00:03:29.850
"Wait a minute, my county lost

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.660
millions of dollars in tax revenue."

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.070
- [Narrator] State
legislators like Ed Soliday,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:38.040
a Republican representing
Valparaiso, Indiana,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.800
say that the restrictions may
harm the region's economy.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:46.130
In 2021, Soliday wrote and
introduced House Bill 1381,

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:47.610
which would have prevented counties

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.810
from issuing outright bans
on wind and solar projects.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.340
- The one thing we know for sure

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.150
is there's a market for renewable energy,

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:00.000
and we can either pay ever
rising transmission costs

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.640
to buy it from someone else,

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.450
or we can participate in
the market here in Indiana.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:06.110
- [Narrator] The bill
introduced by Soliday

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:09.240
died in the state's
2021 legislative session

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.760
as locals pushed back.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.450
He says a similar battle is
unfolding over solar energy.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.670
- That same group that opposed wind

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.590
has now come up with health arguments,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:22.910
visual arguments against solar panels.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.860
And most of those will not
stand up to peer review.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.240
- It's important that
these projects are designed

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.800
to be serving the community as a whole.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.940
Because if they're not designed that way,

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.760
it can lead to downstream flooding.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:41.760
It can lead to resentment
and therefore antagonism,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.440
and therefore opposition
to these projects.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.560
- [Narrator] Jesse Kharbanda
is the executive director

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.490
at the Hoosier Environmental Council.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.610
His group supports renewable energy

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.400
but they argue that locals
should maintain control

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.260
over how development is managed.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.220
- By the end of the decade,

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.170
the overall land footprint
of the solar farms in Indiana

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.010
could be of the size

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.800
of the entire Indiana State Park system.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.260
And so how solar farms are designed

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.770
in the backdrop of climate
change really matters

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:17.090
because they can act as an
agent for community resilience

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:19.750
in the face of more intense
and more frequent flooding,

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:23.380
or they could exacerbate it
if they're designed poorly.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.000
- [Narrator] Critics
say that the solar farms

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.440
will eliminate farmland and
could disrupt natural habitats.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.510
Similar conflicts have
broken out across the plains

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.730
of Kansas and Ohio in the
sunny California desert

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.640
and along the windy
Northern Atlantic coast.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.500
Plans for wind farms in the Northeast

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.320
were introduced in the 2000s.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:42.740
But as of 2021,

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:45.570
little had been built beyond
a five turbine project

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.380
near Rhode Island, that
didn't keep developers

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.310
from pursuing the area.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.470
Biden officials have given final approval

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.310
to a $2.8 billion project
to place 84 turbines,

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.773
12 miles off the coast
of Nantucket island.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.440
Researchers at National
Renewable Energy Laboratory

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.310
are modeling how these political battles

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.430
will affect the country's energy supply.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:08.660
- It's very difficult to
capture people within models.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.620
The social or human side
is always hard to distill

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.190
into a set of equations.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.190
- [Narrator] Trieu Mai is
a senior energy analyst

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.880
at the National Renewable
Energy Laboratory.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.140
His team's research shows
that the US has enough

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.280
wind resources to power the grid,

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.840
but the local opposition movements

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.980
are starting to limit the supply of land

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.170
that can be used for wind farms.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.240
When Mai's team modeled out a scenario

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.910
where populated counties
keep banning renewables

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.480
development was concentrated

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.500
into the relatively
unsettled mountain west.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:41.950
- We never expected land
to be a constraint for wind

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.180
and renewable deployment

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.260
for all the energy needs that we have.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.550
But now that we've
looked into this further

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.790
land use conflicts is a
very important issue here.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.930
- [Narrator] Experts say
that the bans on renewables

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:56.210
will push up the costs
of the energy transition.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.770
That's because wind and solar

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.910
are among the cheapest sources of energy

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.880
according to levelized cost estimates

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:03.580
from the consulting agency Lazard.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.600
Renewables have been deemed critical

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.950
to the future of the global economy,

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.490
but the backlashes unfolding in the US

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.976
show a latent risk as the
green energy markets heat up.

00:07:15.976 --> 00:07:19.143
(lighthearted music)

